  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1009 PM MDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nDRYING OUT FOR THE WEEKEND: \n\nA cold frontal passage will propagate throughout the area late \nFriday night into Saturday morning. This will help remove some of \nthe moisture from the region. With that being said, PWATs are still \nforecast to remain ~150% of normal through Saturday. This means that \nstorm coverage and the threat for heavy rainfall decreases, but \nenough moisture is in place to support isolated to scattered \nconvection Saturday. Current HiRes guidance suggests most of \nthe storm activity to occur along and north of I-70. In \naddition to less coverage of precipitation, temperatures will \ncool by a few degrees. \n\nBy Sunday, PWATs drop to ~75-125% of normal, which indicates there \nmay be enough remnant moisture for isolated convection over the \nhigher terrain. Aside from that, lower elevation areas can expect a \npleasant day with scattered afternoon cloud cover. \n\nRETURN OF PRECIPITATION CHANCES NEXT WEEK:\n\nThe aforementioned drier period won't stick around for long as \nanother pool of rich moisture is advected into the region. Specific \ntiming may change in the coming days as more model guidance becomes \navailable, but it currently looks like this moisture enters the \nregion by Tuesday and sticks around for a few days. The Euro Ens \nappears to be more bullish when it comes to PWAT anomalies, whereas \nthe GFS Ens seems a little less so. Either way, both models are \nhinting at moisture pools favoring the area south of I-70. All that \nbeing said, this event does not appear to be as widespread as what \nwe have experienced this past week.\n\nOn a side note... as moisture returns next week alongside cooler \novernight lows, we could see the first snowflakes of the season over \nour highest peaks, particularly in the San Juans and eastern Uintas.",
